Keep resending a control-channel reliable packet until it is acknowledged.

A reliable packet was resent every 10 ms, at most 20 times, and then dropped. On TV Wi-Fi a
stall over ~200 ms (a background scan or power save, every few minutes) used up all 20 resends,
and the packet was lost. On the control channel that packet carries HID input: a lost one
either blocks the host's ordered channel behind the gap or, once the host skips it, desyncs the
controller state, which freezes input until the stream recovers. Steam's own client resends
until the packet is acknowledged, which is why a Steam Deck on the same Wi-Fi doesn't freeze.

Control-channel reliable packets now keep retransmitting with backoff (10 ms doubling, capped
at 200 ms) until they are acknowledged or the session ends. Other channels still give up after
20 attempts. retransmitCount is a uint8_t on the wire, so it saturates at 255 instead of
wrapping, which would reset the backoff. The 10 s host-silence timeout (patch 0016) still ends
a truly dead session, and session teardown still frees any pending packet (the timer is
destroyed first, running each task's end callback, then Deinit frees the rest).

diff --git a/src/session/retransmission.c b/src/session/retransmission.c
index 5907f74..2b25cfd 100644
--- a/src/session/retransmission.c
+++ b/src/session/retransmission.c
@@ -28,6 +28,9 @@
 
 #define RETRANSMISSION_INTERVAL 10
 #define RETRANSMISSION_ATTEMPTS 20
+#define RETRANSMISSION_INTERVAL_MAX 200
+// retransmitCount is uint8_t on the wire; saturate here so it can't wrap.
+#define RETRANSMISSION_COUNT_MAX 255
 
 /*
  * A pending packet belongs to its timer task: the task's end callback takes it out of the queue
@@ -57,6 +60,28 @@ static bool RetransmissionIdenticalPredicate(PendingRetransmission *item, void *
 
 static bool RetransmissionCumulativePredicate(PendingRetransmission *item, void *context);
 
+// Control-channel reliable packets carry HID input and session control; a lost one desyncs the
+// host (a held button sticks and nothing else gets through), so keep resending until the packet
+// is acked or the session ends, as Steam's own client does. Other channels still give up after
+// RETRANSMISSION_ATTEMPTS. The 10 s host-silence timeout still ends a truly dead session.
+static bool RetransmissionShouldContinue(const IHS_SessionPacket *packet) {
+    if (packet->header.channelId == IHS_SessionChannelIdControl) {
+        return true;
+    }
+    return packet->header.retransmitCount < RETRANSMISSION_ATTEMPTS;
+}
+
+// Back off from 10 ms, doubling, capped at 200 ms, so retransmits during a long outage don't
+// spend the uplink. retransmitCount counts the queued copy as 1, so the first resend waits 10 ms.
+static uint64_t RetransmissionInterval(uint8_t retransmitCount) {
+    unsigned shift = retransmitCount > 0 ? (unsigned) (retransmitCount - 1) : 0;
+    if (shift > 5) {
+        shift = 5;  // 10 << 5 = 320 ms, past the cap anyway
+    }
+    uint64_t interval = (uint64_t) RETRANSMISSION_INTERVAL << shift;
+    return interval > RETRANSMISSION_INTERVAL_MAX ? RETRANSMISSION_INTERVAL_MAX : interval;
+}
+
